    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:gamma-separation",
      "thm:oracle-preservation",
      "thm:gaussian-branch",
      "ass:opposite-overlap"
    ],
    "justification": "The explicit arm partition makes equal, unequal, and mixed regimes coherent without inventing a Hall index for a bounded arm.",
    "gap": "The oracle QTE paper gives joint endpoint laws but not recombination with a shared flexible propensity likelihood.",
    "consumer": "Analysts obtain joint QTE inference when treatment and control have different overlap regimes.",
    "proof_tex": "The definition of \\(\\widehat\\Delta^{\\rm bc}\\) gives the exact identity\n\\[\n\\frac n{H_n}(\\widehat\\Delta^{\\rm bc}-\\Delta)\n=\\ell_{1,n}\\frac n{H_{1,n}}\n\\{\\widehat q_1^{\\rm bc}-q_1\\}\n-\\ell_{0,n}\\frac n{H_{0,n}}\n\\{\\widehat q_0^{\\rm bc}-q_0\\}.\n\\]\nThe armwise limits are supplied by \\(\\text{thm:oracle-preservation}\\) and the narrowed \\(\\text{thm:gaussian-branch}\\).  Since there are only two arms, their remainder terms are jointly \\(o_P(1)\\).  Along a subsequence on which \\(\\ell_{a,n}\\to\\ell_a\\), multiplication by the deterministic convergent coefficients in the last display yields\n\\[\n\\frac n{H_n}(\\widehat\\Delta^{\\rm bc}-\\Delta)\n\\Rightarrow\\ell_1L_1-\\ell_0L_0.\n\\]\n\nFor a heavy arm, the Hall calculation gives\n\\[\nh_{a,n}=(c_an)^{1/\\gamma_a}\\{1+o(1)\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\gamma_1<\\gamma_0<2\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac{h_{1,n}}{h_{0,n}}\n=\\frac{c_1^{1/\\gamma_1}}{c_0^{1/\\gamma_0}}\nn^{1/\\gamma_1-1/\\gamma_0}\\{1+o(1)\\}\n\\longrightarrow\\infty,\n\\]\nso the smaller index dominates.  If \\(\\gamma_1=\\gamma_0=\\gamma\\), the ratio tends to\n\\((c_1/c_0)^{1/\\gamma}\\), so both coefficients have nonzero limits.  Also\n\\[\n\\frac{h_{a,n}}{\\sqrt n}\n=c_a^{1/\\gamma_a}n^{1/\\gamma_a-1/2}\\{1+o(1)\\}\n\\longrightarrow\\infty\n\\quad(\\gamma_a<2),\n\\]\nwhich proves heavy-over-Gaussian dominance.  If both arms are Gaussian, both normalizations equal \\(\\sqrt n\\), and the joint covariance is the covariance of the two influence-function vectors; the common fitted likelihood contribution is therefore retained rather than discarded.\n\nIt remains to justify the asserted independence in the two-heavy oracle-preserved case.  Binary propensity coherence and \\(\\text{ass:opposite-overlap}\\) give disjoint fixed neighborhoods of the two zero-propensity endpoints.  For nonnegative compactly supported test functions \\(g_0,g_1\\) on the two endpoint charts, independence of observations gives a joint Laplace transform of the form\n\\[\n\\left[\n1-\\frac{\\nu_{0,n}(1-e^{-g_0})}{n}\n-\\frac{\\nu_{1,n}(1-e^{-g_1})}{n}\n+o(n^{-1})\n\\right]^n.\n\\]\nThe two rare events cannot occur for the same observation, so there is no cross term of order \\(n^{-1}\\).  The Hall and mark limits make\n\\(\\nu_{a,n}(1-e^{-g_a})\\to\\nu_a(1-e^{-g_a})\\); hence the display converges to\n\\[\n\\exp\\{-\\nu_0(1-e^{-g_0})\\}\n\\exp\\{-\\nu_1(1-e^{-g_1})\\}.\n\\]\nThis factorization proves independence of the two endpoint Poisson limits.  The finite-arm oracle-transfer errors are jointly \\(o_P(1)\\), so the same independence holds for the two oracle-preserved feasible endpoint processes.",
